{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Ice Edge Diatom Bloom: Bering Sea Spring Retreat",
    "date": "2026-08-28",
    "lesson": "As Bering Sea pack ice retreats each spring, meltwater stabilizes a thin, brightly lit surface layer over deep, nutrient-rich water. Diatoms bloom explosively where light and nutrients briefly coexist, and their die-off feeds a wave of copiotrophic bacteria (like Pseudoalteromonas) that digest the polysaccharide-rich exudate and dead frustules. This scenario shows carbohydrate-dominated food (diatom exudate and cell walls) driving a bacterial succession right at the ice edge, while the bloom itself sinks, dies, and refuels the water column from above.",
    "citation": "Based on Bering Sea shelf ice-edge bloom dynamics (e.g. BEST/BSIERP program observations).",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Bering Sea ice-edge phytoplankton bloom and associated bacterial succession"
  },
